---
import { Code } from '@astrojs/starlight/components';
import Callout from '../docs/Callout.astro';
---

<div class="property-section">
  <Code
    code={`"channels": [
    {
        "id": "channelName",
        "label": { "name": "Channel Name", "prefix": "", "suffix": "" },
        "event": "valueChanged",
        "type": "number",
        "range": {"min":0, "max":100, "defaultValue":0, "skew":1, "increment":0.1}
    }
]`}
    lang="json"
  />
  <p>
    In Cabbage 3, widgets use an array of channels instead of a single channel string. Each channel object includes an
    <code>id</code> that defines a unique channel name. Widgets in Cabbage 3 can have any number of channels attached to
    them. An optional <code>label</code> object controls how the parameter appears in DAW parameter editors and
    automation lanes; if omitted, the <code>id</code> is used. An optional <code>event</code> property specifies the
    type of interaction. A <code>range</code> object can define the minimum, maximum, and default values, along with the
    skew and step increment. The <code>type</code> is set to <code>number</code> by default. Each widget sets its own
    type, <code>number</code> or <code>string</code>. A <code>"string"</code> channel carries text (readable with the
    string variant of <code>cabbageGetValue</code>), persists through session saves, and is excluded from DAW automation.
    See <a href="/docs/custom-ui/persisting-ui-state/">Persisting Custom UI State</a>.
  </p>

  <Callout type="caution" title="Two different labels">
    <p>
      The <code>label</code> object inside <code>channels[]</code> only sets the name shown in DAW parameter lists and
      automation lanes. It never changes any on-screen text. The text a widget displays on screen is set with the
      widget's top-level <code>Label</code> property (see below):
    </p>
    <Code
      code={`"label": {"text": "Cutoff"},
"channels": [{"id": "cutoff", "label": {"name": "Filter Cutoff"}}]`}
      lang="json"
    />
    <p>Csound code always uses the channel <code>id</code>, never either label.</p>
  </Callout>

  <p><strong>Channel label: DAW display name</strong></p>
  <p>
    The <code>label</code> object separates the internal channel identifier from the display name shown in DAWs, and
    allows a prefix and suffix to be appended to the parameter's value string in the DAW. This is useful when you want
    short, code-friendly channel IDs (e.g., <code>hrm1</code>, <code>hrm2</code>) while showing descriptive names in the
    DAW (e.g., <code>Harmonic 1</code>, <code>Harmonic 2</code>). Csound code always uses the <code>id</code> for
    channel access via <code>cabbageGetValue</code> and <code>cabbageSetValue</code>.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Field</th><th>Description</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>name</code></td>
        <td>Human-readable parameter name shown in the DAW (required if using <code>label</code>)</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>prefix</code></td>
        <td>String prepended to the value in the DAW's value display (optional)</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>suffix</code></td>
        <td>String appended to the value in the DAW's value display (optional)</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <Callout type="caution">
    <p>
      During runtime, range objects will be appended with the current value. You should never set this value explicitly
      as it will override any state recall.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <p>
    If range is omitted, it defaults to a range from 0 to 1, with increments of 0.001 for widgets that use drag events
    (e.g., sliders, XY pads) and 1 for widgets that use click or toggle events (e.g., buttons, checkboxes).
  </p>
  <p><strong>Note on <code>increment</code> parameter behavior:</strong></p>
  <p>
    The <code>increment</code> parameter quantizes all values sent to Csound channels, ensuring they snap to the
    specified step size. For example, with <code>increment: 0.1</code>, values will be rounded to 0.0, 0.1, 0.2, etc.
    before reaching Csound.
  </p>
  <p>The DAW host automation has additional constraints:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong><code>increment &gt;= 1.0</code></strong>: The host will display discrete integer steps (e.g., move from 1
      to 2 to 3, etc.) in its native controls, and enforce these steps during automation.
    </li>
    <li><strong><code>increment &lt; 1.0</code></strong>: The host displays a continuous slider.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Cabbage always quantizes values internally before sending to Csound.</p>

  <Callout>
    <p>
      A widget's top-level id property defines the general communication channel for updating attributes, while the
      channels specified in the channels array are used for value and parameter updates. If no top level id is
      specified, the first channel.id will be used as the general communication channel.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <p>Supported event types:</p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Event</th><th>Description</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>valueChanged</code> (default)</td><td>Tracks simple value changes</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>mousePressLeft</code></td><td>Tracks presses of left mouse button</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>mousePressRight</code></td><td>Tracks presses of right mouse button</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>mousePressMiddle</code></td><td>Tracks presses of middle mouse button</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>mouseReleaseLeft</code></td><td>Tracks releases of left mouse button</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>mouseReleaseRight</code></td><td>Tracks releases of right mouse button</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>mouseReleaseMiddle</code></td><td>Tracks releases of middle mouse button</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>mouseMoveX</code></td><td>Tracks movement along X axis.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>mouseMoveY</code></td><td>Tracks movement along Y axis.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>mouseDragX</code></td><td>Tracks movement along X axis with mouse pressed</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>mouseDragY</code></td><td>Tracks movement along Y axis with mouse pressed</td></tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>mouseInside</code></td>
        <td>Sends the channel maximum while the pointer is inside the widget and the minimum when it leaves.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <Callout>
    <p>In Cabbage 3, channels don't need to start with a letter and white spaces are permitted.</p>
  </Callout>
</div>
